the Pentagon says it successfully tested  a missile defense system that could  protect the United States from North  Korea the military first fired a target  missile yesterday from a remote island  in the Pacific then it launched an  interceptor missile from California to  destroy it and video shows the  interceptor launched the ability to  shoot down an intercontinental missile  comes just days after the latest North  Korean missile test David Martin is at  the Pentagon David good morning good  morning since North Korea has said it is  finishing work on an intercontinental  ballistic missile this was an important  demonstration that the US has the  capability at least under test  conditions to shoot down an incoming  ICBM the target a missile with the  trajectory and speed of an ICBM was  launched from the Kwajalein Atoll in the  Pacific minutes later an interceptor  missile blasted off from Vandenberg Air  Force Base in California maneuvering  into the path of the incoming ICBM  traveling at roughly four miles per  second according to the Pentagon's  missile defense agency the kill vehicle  intercepted and destroyed the target in  a direct collision 36 interceptors are  based in silos in California and Alaska  they go on alert every time the u.s.  expects another missile test by North  Korea but until now the system had never  been tested against a missile capable of  threatening the US homeland with a  nuclear warhead this test which costs  244 million dollars and was nearly three  years in the planning brings the overall  success rate to ten intercepts in 18  tries a modest record even though test  conditions allow time to move powerful  radars like this sea-based x-band radar  into position Tomasz Caraco is with the  Center for Strategic and International  Studies the most realistic test the only  true realistic test would be if North  Korea were to launch something at the  United States nobody wants that kind of  realism if it were the real thing with  little or no warning the US would have  to launch multiple interceptors against  an incoming missile so if one missed the  second or third might hit and so in that  sense you also kind of reduced  reduce the risk of any one particular  interceptor failing and you kind of  increase the effectiveness the Blakely  hood you're going to kill it it's hard  to think of the United States and an  arms race with an impoverished country  like North Korea but that is essentially  what's happening u.s. missile defense is  racing to keep a head of North Korean  nuclear missiles Gayle it is hard to  think of it in those terms thank you  very much David
 

